      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Admission hyperglycaemia is associated with poorer prognosis in patients with an acute coronary syndrome (ACS). Whether hyperglycaemia is more important than prior long-term glucose metabolism, is unknown."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "AIM",
          "content": "To investigate the prognostic value of admission glucose and HbA(1c) levels in patients with ACS."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "We measured glucose and HbA(1c) at admission in 521 consecutive patients with suspected ACS. Glucose was categorized as <7.8 (n = 305), 7.8-11.0 (n = 138) or > or =11.1 mmol/l (n = 78); HbA(1c) as <6.2% (n = 420) or > or =6.2% (n = 101). Mean follow-up was 1.6 +/- 0.5 years."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The diagnosis of ACS was confirmed in 332 patients (64%), leaving 189 (36%) with atypical chest pain. In ACS patients, mortality by glucose category (<7.8, 7.8-11.0 or > or =11.1 mmol) was 9%, 8% and 25%, respectively (p = 0.001); mortality by HbA(1c) category (<6.2% vs. > or =6.2%) was 10% vs. 17%, respectively (p = 0.14). On multivariate analysis, glucose category was significantly associated with mortality (HR 3.0, 95% CI 1.1-8.3), but HbA(1c) category was not (HR 1.5, 95%CI 0.6-4.2)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "DISCUSSION",
          "content": "Elevated admission glucose appears more important than prior long-term abnormal glucose metabolism in predicting mortality in patients with suspected ACS."
        }
      ]},
